\nthe same up to GL-equivalence.\n20--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGL-equivalence conjecture for sandpile groups of Cayley graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344907/gl-equivalence-conjecture-for-sandpile-groups-of-cayley-grap)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # GL-equivalence conjecture for sandpile groups of Cayley graphs ... The two Cayley graphs have the same sandpile group if and only if their generator multiplicities are the same up to $GL$G L-equivalence.\n\n# GL-equivalence conjecture for sandpile groups of Cayley graphs\n\nAbout 7 years old · traced to 2019\n\nLet two Cayley graphs have generator multiplicities, and let $GL$G L-equivalence mean equivalence under the natural action of the relevant general linear group on the generators. GL-equivalence conjecture. The two Cayley graphs have the same sandpile group if and only if their generator multiplicities are the same up to $GL$G L-equivalence.\n\nThis proposes a classification of the sandpile group by the linear-equivalence class of the generator multiplicities. The source lists it among remaining conjectures based on data.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJiyang Gao, Jared Marx-Kuo, Vaughan McDonald and Chi Ho Yuen, “Sandpile Groups of Cayley Graphs of F_2^r”, arXiv:1912.06919 (2024).\n\ncayley-graphslinear-algebrasandpile-groups\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Sandpile Group of Cayley Graphs (https://jiyanggao.scholars.harvard.edu/sites/g/files/omnuum1121/files/jiyanggao/files/sandpile_poster.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; The Sandpile Group of Cayley Graphs ... The sandpile group of a graph is a ﬁnite abelian ... 2 We conjecture that the eigenvalues of the\nThe Sandpile Group of Cayley Graphs\nJared Marx-Kuo, Jiyang Gao, and Vaughan McDonald\nJoint Mathematics Meetings, January 16-19, 2019, Baltimore, MD\nIntroduction\nPrevious Results\nLimits of Main Results I\nLimits of Main Results II\nPreviously, it has been proven:\n1 For p ̸= 2 we have\n\u0011\nSylp(K(G)) ≃Sylp\n\u0010\n⊕u∈Fr\n2Zλu,M\nOther r = 3 cases and r = 4 appear far less\ntractable, since in these cases Syl2(K(G)) has at\nleast 5 cyclic factors, so we would need to explic-\nitly compute 4 factors by hand. This was reasonable\nwhen we had the symmetry of the hypercube, but\nin general appears to be diﬃcult.\n2 [2] Let ck(G) denote the exponent of the kth\nlargest cyclic factor in Syl2(K(G)). Then\nc1(Qn) ≤n + ⌊log2 n⌋\nWe are unable to determine ck(Qn) for k ≥n be-\ncause cn(Qn) is the additive order of some x1x2−1+\nt1(x1 −1)+t2(x2 −1), and its value is more diﬃcult\nto determine number-theoretically. However, based\non data, we conjecture that for n ≥3, cn(Qn) =\nmax{maxx<n−1{v2(x) + x}, v2(n −1) + n −3}. and\nfor n ≥4 that cn+1(Qn) = maxx<n−1{v2(x) + x}.\nWe have no conjectures for further factors.\nConclusion and Remaining\nQuestions\nThe sandpile group of a graph is a ﬁnite abelian\ngroup with combinatorial, algebraic, and geometric\ninterpretations. We are interested in the sandpile\ngroup of hypercube graphs and their generalizations,\nCayley graphs of the group Fr\n2 with arbitrary gen-\nerating sets. While the Sylow-p component of these\nsandpile groups has been classiﬁed for p ̸= 2 [1],\nthe Sylow-2 subgroup remains a mystery. In this\nproject, we use linear algebra and ring theory to\nachieve the following results:\nMain Results I: Largest Sylow-2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGao–Marx-Kuo–McDonald–Yuen, Conjectures 4.14 and 5.4 (sandpile group of the hypercube) — solved, with a Lean 4 proof · Issue #7169 · google-deepmind/formal-conjectures · GitHub (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/issues/7169)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Betzer (Sandpile groups of Cayley graphs over 𝔽_2^r: Sylow-2 structure, isospectral counterexamples, and Boolean incidence algebra, preprint, Zenodo, August 2026, doi:10.5281/zenodo.22065778), through Remark 4.13 of Gao, Marx-Kuo, McDonald and Yuen, which they state without proof. ... The sandpile group of a finite graph (cokernel of the reduced Laplacian, or the torsion of the cokernel of the Laplacian), and the hypercube graph, which this repository already defines (`SimpleGraph.hypercube` in `FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Hypercube.lean`). ... A correction of attribution: Conjecture 4.14 had been proved earlier, on paper, by D.\n\n# Gao–Marx-Kuo–McDonald–Yuen, Conjectures 4.14 and 5.4 (sandpile group of the hypercube) — solved, with a Lean 4 proof #7169\n\nGao–Marx-Kuo–McDonald–Yuen, Conjectures 4.14 and 5.4 (sandpile group of the hypercube) — solved, with a Lean 4 proof#7169\n\nLabels\n\nnew conjectureIssues about open conjectures/unsolved problems problem. Category `research open`Issues about open conjectures/unsolved problems problem. Category `research open`\n\n\n### What is the conjecture\n\nLet `K(Q_n)` be the sandpile (critical) group of the `n`-dimensional hypercube graph `Q_n`, and `Syl_2` its Sylow 2-subgroup. For a finite abelian 2-group, let `c_r` be the exponent `e` of the `r`-th largest cyclic factor `ℤ/2^e`.\n\nJ. Gao, J. Marx-Kuo, V. McDonald, C. H. Yuen, Sandpile groups of Cayley graphs of 𝔽_2^r, arXiv:1912.06919 (v3), state two conjectures:\n\n  * Conjecture 4.14. For `n ≥ 3`, `v_2(c_n(Q_n)) = max( max_{x<n−1} (v_2(x) + x), v_2(n−1) + n − 3 )`.\n  * Conjecture 5.4. For `k ≥ 1`, `Syl_2 K(Q_{2^k}) ≅ Syl_2 K(Q_{2^k−1})^2 × ℤ/2^{2^k+k−1}`.\n\n### Prerequisites needed\n\nThe sandpile group of a finite graph (cokernel of the reduced Laplacian, or the torsion of the cokernel of the Laplacian), and the hypercube graph, which this repository already defines (`SimpleGraph.hypercube` in `FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Hypercube.lean`). In the Lean project above, `K(Q_n)` is defined as the torsion subgroup of the cokernel of the Laplacian `f ↦ n·f(g) − Σ_i f(g + e_i)` on the functions `(ℤ/2)^n → ℤ` (`CubeLaplacian.K`); the definitions are printed verbatim in §1.2 of the certificate.\n\n### AMS categories\n\n  * ams-05 (Combinatorics)\n  * ams-20 (Group theory and generalizations)\n\n### Choose either option\n\n  * [Input] I plan on adding this conjecture to the repository\n  * [Input] This issue is up for grabs: I would like to see this conjecture added by somebody else\n\n  2. ### tretoef-estrella commented on Oct 14, 2026\n\ntretoef-estrella\n\non Oct 14, 2026\n\nAuthor\n\nMore actions\n\nA correction of attribution: Conjecture 4.14 had been proved earlier, on paper, by D. N. Betzer (Sandpile groups of Cayley graphs over 𝔽_2^r: Sylow-2 structure, isospectral counterexamples, and Boolean incidence algebra, preprint, Zenodo, August 2026, doi:10.5281/zenodo.22065778), through Remark 4.13 of Gao, Marx-Kuo, McDonald and Yuen, which they state without proof. We learned of his paper on 13 October 2026. The Lean proof cited above (`PodiumCor.G2`) does not use that remark; version 11 of the paper credits him: doi:10.5281/zenodo.23356712. Nothing else in the issue changes.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSandpile Groups of Cayley Graphs of $\\mathbb{F}_2^r$ - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1912.06919v3)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Comments: v2: 19 pages, 1 figure, 1 table; proved Conjecture 6.1 in older version (now Theorem 2.9), change of authorship. v3: 20 pages, 1 figure, 1 table; minor r